**Describe the bug**
I am trying to adjust the response for errors provided by quarkus extensions but it does not seem to be possible to capture those in ExceptionMappers.
Also, this issue (https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/issues/3253) mentions that `WebApplicationExceptions` works, but that's not the case for me. So I cannot easily capture javax.ws.rs.core.Response exceptions easily.

**Expected behavior**
For example, I would expect to be able to capture hibernate exceptions with either a `ExceptionMapper<PersistenceException>` or `ExceptionMapper<RollbackException>`.

**Actual behavior**

For example, violating a database constraint produces the stacktrace:
```
RuntimeException 
-> javax.transaction.RollBackException 
-> javax.persistence.PersistenceException 
-> org.hibernate.exception.ConstraintViolationException
```
But I am only able to capture it with a `ExceptionMapper<Exception>`.

**To Reproduce**
1. Build a project with `hibernate-orm`
2. Create a service to insert the following entity twice.

```
@Entity
@Table(name = "DEMO")
@Data
public class Demo {
	@Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)
    private Long id;

    @Column(unique = true)
    private String name;
}
```

3. Add the following ExceptionMappers to the project

```java
@Slf4j
@Provider
public class PersistenceExceptionMapper implements ExceptionMapper<PersistenceException> {

    @Override
    public Response toResponse(PersistenceException exception) {
        logger.error("PersistenceException error", exception);
        return Response.status(500)
            .entity(Json.createObjectBuilder()
                .add("message", exception.getMessage())
                .build())
            .build();
    }

}
```

```java
@Slf4j
@Provider
public class RollbackExceptionMapper implements ExceptionMapper<RollbackException> {

    @Override
    public Response toResponse(RollbackException exception) {
        logger.error("RollbackException error", exception);
        return Response.status(500)
            .entity(Json.createObjectBuilder()
                .add("message", exception.getMessage())
                .build())
            .build();
    }

}
```

You will see none of them triggers and the response is a HTML, if in DEV mode.


